@startuml sd-failure-recovery
skinparam shadowing false
title SD - Failure recovery (broker down, poison message, instance down)

participant "Client" as C
participant "airports-routes-1\n(command side)" as A1
database "airports-db-1\n(outbox)" as DB1
participant "AmqpReplicationPublisher\n(every 2 s)" as P
queue "RabbitMQ cluster\nairports.events" as MQ
queue "airports.events.airports-routes-2" as Q2
queue "airports.events.airports-routes-2.dlq" as DLQ
participant "airports-routes-2\nReplicationEventConsumer" as A2

== 1. Broker unavailable: writes keep working ==
C -> A1 : POST /api/airports
A1 -> DB1 : save aggregate + read model\n+ outbox task (PENDING)\n(one transaction)
A1 --> C : 201 Created
P -> DB1 : PENDING tasks (max 100)
P -> MQ : send + waitForConfirms(5 s)
MQ -[#red]-> P : unavailable / no confirm
P -> DB1 : attempts++, nextAttemptAt = now + min(60, 5 << attempts) s
note right of P
  After 10 failed attempts the task becomes FAILED
  (it stays in the table and is visible in
  GET /api/admin/replication-tasks).
end note
... broker back ...
P -> MQ : send + waitForConfirms
MQ --> P : confirm (ack)
P -> DB1 : task = DONE

== 2. Instance 2 is down: events wait in its queue ==
MQ -> Q2 : routed copy (durable, quorum)
note right of Q2 : buffered until instance 2 returns\n(message TTL 1 h, max 10000 messages)
... instance 2 restarts ...
Q2 -> A2 : deliver
A2 -> A2 : apply event + record processed_event\n(one transaction)
A2 --> Q2 : ack

== 3. Poison message: retry, then dead-letter ==
Q2 -> A2 : deliver
A2 -[#red]> A2 : processing fails
A2 -> A2 : retry (listener retry,\n3 attempts: 1 s, then x2)
A2 --> Q2 : reject without requeue\n(AmqpRejectAndDontRequeueException)
Q2 -> DLQ : dead-letter\n(also on TTL expiry or overflow)
note right of DLQ : kept for inspection in the\nRabbitMQ management UI

== 4. Duplicate delivery (at-least-once): applied only once ==
Q2 -> A2 : deliver (same eventId again,\ne.g. ack lost or publisher retry)
A2 -> A2 : processed_event already has eventId\n-> skip (inbox)
A2 --> Q2 : ack (no second effect)
@enduml
